# Day 29 — Wazuh Linux Persistence Detection & Startup Monitoring 🛡️

## 🎯 Objective

Today I practiced using Wazuh to investigate Linux persistence mechanisms and unexpected startup changes from a SOC analyst's perspective.

### Topics covered

- Wazuh File Integrity Monitoring (FIM)
- Linux cron jobs
- systemd services and timers
- SSH authorized keys
- File change detection
- Security event investigation
- SOC incident reporting

> **Safety:** Use only your own Linux VM or an authorized lab. Do not create unauthorized persistence or modify critical system files.

---

## 1. Understand the Detection Workflow

```text
Linux Endpoint
      ↓
Wazuh Agent
      ↓
FIM / Log Collection
      ↓
Wazuh Server Rules
      ↓
Wazuh Indexer
      ↓
Wazuh Dashboard
      ↓
SOC Investigation
```

Wazuh can report changes to monitored files. It does not automatically classify every cron job or service as malicious. The analyst must investigate the change and its context.

---

## 2. Check Your Wazuh Agent

On the monitored Ubuntu endpoint, run:

```bash
sudo systemctl status wazuh-agent --no-pager
```

Check recent agent logs:

```bash
sudo tail -n 50 /var/ossec/logs/ossec.log
```

Confirm that the endpoint is connected to your Wazuh server.

If your Indexer is still failing, continue with local evidence collection, but do not claim that dashboard alerts were verified.

---

## 3. Inspect Linux Persistence Locations

Run the following commands on your Ubuntu endpoint.

### Cron jobs

```bash
crontab -l
```

```bash
sudo crontab -l
```

```bash
ls -la /etc/cron.d/
```

### systemd services

```bash
systemctl list-unit-files --type=service
```

### systemd timers

```bash
systemctl list-timers --all
```

### SSH authorized keys

```bash
cat ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
```

If the file does not exist, that is not necessarily an error.

These commands help you establish what is currently configured. Wazuh FIM will only detect changes to paths that are appropriately monitored.

---

## 4. Check Wazuh FIM Configuration

On the endpoint, inspect:

```bash
sudo grep -A30 -B5 '<syscheck>' /var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf
```

Look for the `<syscheck>` section and its monitored directories.

Wazuh FIM commonly monitors configured paths such as `/etc`, depending on the installation and configuration. Do not assume every persistence location is covered.

If you change the configuration, back it up first and preserve the existing XML structure.

```bash
sudo cp /var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf \
/var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf.bak
```

---

## 5. Configure a Safe FIM Test Directory

We will monitor a test directory instead of changing real startup mechanisms.

Create the directory:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/wazuh-fim-lab
```

Create a test file:

```bash
echo "Original configuration" > ~/wazuh-fim-lab/test.txt
```

Check its hash:

```bash
sha256sum ~/wazuh-fim-lab/test.txt
```

Find your username:

```bash
whoami
```

Edit the Wazuh agent configuration:

```bash
sudo nano /var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf
```

Inside the existing `<syscheck>` section, add a monitored directory entry, replacing `YOUR_USERNAME`:

```xml
<directories check_all="yes">/home/YOUR_USERNAME/wazuh-fim-lab</directories>
```

Do not add a duplicate `<syscheck>` section. If the directory is already monitored, you do not need to add it again.

Save the configuration.

If you made a local configuration change, restart the agent:

```bash
sudo systemctl restart wazuh-agent
```

Verify the service:

```bash
sudo systemctl status wazuh-agent --no-pager
```

